export const MH6={title:'Safe and responsible messaging',hook:'A post meant to help can accidentally harm—by describing a suicide method, glamorizing an eating disorder or sharing a classmate’s story without permission. Mental health messaging has well-established safety guidelines. Following them protects your peers, and judges notice teams that take safety seriously.',objectives:['Apply safe messaging guidelines for suicide and self-harm.','Use strengths-based, hopeful and accurate messages.','Protect privacy and obtain permission for photos, videos and stories.','Make content accessible and manage comments safely.'],sections:[
['Safe messaging about suicide and self-harm',[
'Safe messaging guidelines, used by journalists and prevention organizations, recommend that messages **do not** describe **methods** or **locations** of suicide or self-harm, **do not** show graphic images, **do not** present suicide as a solution to problems or as caused by a single event and **do not** use sensational language such as “epidemic” or “committed suicide.” Instead, say “**died by suicide**.”',
'Messages **should** emphasize that **help is available**, that **suicide is preventable**, that **treatment works** and that people **recover**. Include warning signs and **specific resources**—the **988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline**, the school counselor and how to reach them—in posts about these topics. Review every post with your consultant.',
"These guidelines apply even to well-meant awareness posts. Stories that focus on how a person died, or that portray suicide as romantic or inevitable, can increase risk for vulnerable viewers. Stories of people who struggled, reached out and found help have the opposite effect and are among the most protective messages you can share.",
sort('Safe or unsafe message?',['Safe','Unsafe'],[['“Help is available—call or text 988 any time.”',0],['Describing how someone harmed themselves',1],['“Recovery is possible; talking to someone is a first step.”',0],['“He committed suicide because he failed a test.”',1],['Listing warning signs and how to help a friend',0],['Posting a photo of where a death occurred',1]])]],
['Strengths-based, hopeful messages',[
'Effective prevention messaging is **strengths-based**: it focuses on what people **can do**—coping skills, reaching out, supporting friends—rather than only on problems. It is **hopeful** and shows **positive role models** of help-seeking. Fear-based messages (“If you don’t get help, you’ll end up in the hospital”) can increase shame and avoidance.',
'Keep messages **accurate**, citing reputable sources, and **simple**: one main idea per post with a clear **call to action** (“Save this list of coping strategies,” “Text a friend you haven’t talked to in a while,” “Visit the counseling office in room 112”). Use **person-first, non-stigmatizing language**.',
"Hopeful does not mean unrealistic. Avoid promising that a single tip will “fix” anxiety or depression. Instead, present coping strategies as tools that can help, alongside the message that it is okay to need more support and that reaching out to a professional is a sign of strength.",
sort('Strengths-based or fear-based?',['Strengths-based','Fear-based'],[['“Three ways to calm your mind before a test”',0],['“Ignore stress and your life will fall apart”',1],['“Reaching out takes courage—here’s how”',0],['“Mental illness ruins futures”',1],['“Check on a friend today”',0]])]],
['Privacy, consent and permissions',[
'Get **written permission** before featuring anyone in photos, videos or interviews, and a **parent or guardian’s permission** for minors when your school requires it. Never post someone’s **personal mental health story** without their clear consent, and consider whether sharing could harm them later. Fictional scenarios or general statements often work better than real disclosures.',
'Follow your school’s **media policy**. Use **original content** or content you have the right to use (properly licensed images and music); credit sources. The guidelines say **not to post on personal accounts**; create a **dedicated account** for the campaign or use your chapter’s HOSA account. Judges should never need to view personal pages.',
"If you interview a professional, such as your consultant or a coach, ask for permission to record and share the clip, and let them review how it will be used. Respecting everyone who appears in the campaign builds trust with your school community.",
match('Situation and responsible choice',[['Featuring a classmate in a video','Get written permission'],['Sharing a friend’s story','Only with clear consent, or use a fictional example'],['Using a popular song','Use licensed or royalty-free music'],['Posting the campaign','Use a dedicated or chapter account'],['Student under 18 on camera','Follow school rules on guardian permission'],['Using an image found online','Check the license and credit it']])]],
['Accessibility and inclusion',[
'Make content usable by everyone. Add **captions** to videos, **alt text** to images, and use **high color contrast** and readable font sizes. Avoid flashing animations. Use **plain language** and consider translating key posts into languages spoken in your school community.',
'Represent your school’s **diversity** respectfully. Mental health experiences differ by culture, identity and background, and some groups face extra barriers such as discrimination or lack of access. Ask your consultant how to address these respectfully and avoid stereotypes.',
order('Accessibility checklist for each post',['Captions on any video','Alt text on images','High-contrast colors','Readable font size','Plain language','No flashing effects'])]],
['Managing comments and responses',[
'The HOSA General Rules include guidance on **disabling comments** on social media posts. Turning off comments prevents harmful replies, bullying or crisis disclosures that your team cannot safely handle. If comments are allowed by your school, a **trusted adult** should monitor them.',
'Have a **response plan** with your consultant: if a peer messages your account saying they are struggling, the team should **not** try to counsel them. Respond with care and resources (“Thank you for reaching out. Please talk to a trusted adult or call or text 988”) and **notify your consultant or advisor** right away, following school policy.',
chk('A student sends your campaign account a message saying they feel hopeless. What should the team do?',['Reply with care and resources and notify your consultant or advisor right away','Try to counsel the student yourselves','Ignore it','Share the message publicly'],'Have a response plan and involve adults.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Reviewing a draft post',{a:{text:'A draft post shares statistics about teen suicide and the word “epidemic” in large red letters. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you change?',choices:[['Remove sensational wording, add warning signs, hope and 988','b','Correct.',true],['Make the font bigger','b','The issue is unsafe messaging.',false]]},b:{text:'A teammate wants to include a friend’s real story of self-harm. What do you do?',choices:[['Use a general or fictional example unless the friend gives clear consent and the consultant approves; never include method details','c','Correct.',true],['Post it—real stories get more views','c','Privacy and safety come first.',false]]},c:{text:'Your consultant approves the revised post. You disable comments and schedule it.',end:true}})]],
["Messaging about other sensitive topics",["**Eating and body image:** avoid numbers (weights, calories, sizes), “before and after” photos, food labels such as “good” and “bad,” and comments about anyone’s body. Focus on body respect, balanced eating, the joy of movement and where to get help. The National Eating Disorders resources and your consultant can guide wording.","**Substance use:** avoid images of people using substances, which can normalize them, and avoid scare tactics. Share accurate effects on the developing brain, refusal skills, healthy alternatives and how to get help. **Bullying and violence:** avoid naming individuals or schools; focus on bystander skills, reporting and support for those affected.","For any topic, ask: Is this **accurate**? Could it **trigger** someone who is struggling? Does it **include hope** and a **resource**? Would I be comfortable if the person most affected by this topic saw it?",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Topic and safe approach","pairs":[["Body image","No weights, calories or before-and-after photos"],["Vaping","Accurate brain effects and refusal skills"],["Bullying","Bystander skills and reporting"],["Grief and loss","Coping, support and resources"],["Self-harm","Healthy coping and help-seeking; no images or methods"],["Any topic","Accurate, hopeful and includes a resource"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Safe messaging: never describe methods, locations or graphic images; avoid sensational words; say “died by suicide”; emphasize that help is available, treatment works and recovery is possible; include 988 and school resources.','Use strengths-based, hopeful, accurate messages with one idea and a clear call to action; get written permission for people and stories; use original or licensed content; post on a dedicated or chapter account, not personal accounts.','Add captions, alt text and high contrast; represent diversity respectfully; disable or monitor comments; have a response plan to share resources and notify your consultant if a peer reaches out.'],terms:[['Safe messaging','Guidelines for communicating about suicide and mental health in ways that reduce risk.'],['Call to action','A specific step you ask your audience to take.'],['Alt text','A written description of an image for people using screen readers.']],mcqs:[q('Which phrase follows safe messaging guidelines?','Died by suicide','Committed suicide','Successful suicide','Suicide epidemic','Avoid stigmatizing wording.'),q('Which should never appear in a campaign?','Methods of self-harm','The 988 number','Warning signs','Coping strategies','Unsafe content.'),q('A strengths-based message focuses on:','What people can do','Fear of consequences','Shame','Statistics only','Hopeful.'),q('Where should the campaign be posted?','A dedicated or chapter account','A team member’s personal account','A private message','Nowhere','Per the guidelines.'),q('What should you get before featuring a classmate in a video?','Written permission','Nothing','A grade','A fee','Consent.'),q('Which improves accessibility?','Captions on videos','Flashing animations','Tiny fonts','Low contrast','Usable by all.'),q('Why might teams disable comments?','To prevent harmful replies and unsafe disclosures','To hide the campaign','Because judges require likes','To increase views','GRR guidance.'),q('If a peer messages the account in distress, the team should:','Share resources and notify the consultant or advisor','Counsel them alone','Ignore it','Post the message','Response plan.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would make sure a campaign about suicide prevention is safe, respectful and accessible.',rubric:[['Safe messaging',['methods','graphic','sensational','died by suicide','help is available','recovery','988']],['Positive approach',['strengths-based','hopeful','call to action','accurate','person-first']],['Privacy and permissions',['permission','consent','fictional','dedicated account','licensed','personal accounts']],['Access and response',['captions','alt text','contrast','comments','disable','response plan','consultant']]],model:'We would follow safe messaging guidelines in every post: never describing methods or locations, avoiding graphic images and sensational words like “epidemic,” saying “died by suicide” rather than “committed,” and never suggesting suicide is caused by one event or solves problems. Instead, we would emphasize that suicide is preventable, help is available, treatment works and people recover, and we would share warning signs, how to ask a friend directly and specific resources—our counseling office and the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline—in every post. Our messages would be strengths-based and hopeful, with one clear call to action each, such as saving a list of coping strategies or checking on a friend, using accurate information from reputable sources and person-first language. To protect privacy, we would get written permission before featuring anyone, use general or fictional examples instead of real personal stories and use only original or licensed images and music, posting on a dedicated campaign account rather than our personal accounts. For accessibility, we would caption videos, add alt text, use high contrast and readable fonts and avoid flashing effects. We would disable comments to prevent harmful replies and create a response plan with our consultant so that if a peer reached out in distress, we would respond with care and resources and immediately notify our consultant or advisor.'},sources:[RULES,SAFE_MSG,LIFELINE,WAI]};

export const MH7={title:'Designing campaign content that reaches peers',hook:'Peers scroll fast. A post has about a second to earn attention and a few more to deliver a message worth saving or sharing. Judges score effectiveness, peer education, design and creativity—75 of the 115 Round One points. This lesson covers how to plan, create and schedule content your classmates will actually stop for.',objectives:['Know your audience and choose platforms and formats.','Write strong hooks, clear messages and calls to action.','Apply design principles for organized, error-free content.','Build a content calendar and timeline.'],sections:[
['Know your audience and platform',[
'Your audience is your **peers**—students at your school or in your community. Learn what they use and like: Instagram, TikTok, YouTube, Snapchat or school websites. Ask a few students what kinds of posts they stop for. Content should sound like it was made **for teens, by teens**, while staying accurate and respectful.',
'Choose **formats** that fit your message: **short videos** and reels for demonstrations (a breathing exercise), **carousels** for step-by-step tips, **infographics** for quick facts, **interviews** with a counselor or coach, **polls** and **challenges** to boost participation, **animations** or **cartoons** for sensitive topics and **blog posts** for more depth. A mix keeps the campaign interesting.',
"Think about how each platform is used. Stories disappear after a day, so save important content as posts or highlights that judges can still view. Reels and short videos reach new viewers. Longer explanations fit a blog or website. Whatever you choose, make sure the links will still work when judges review them.",
match('Message and best format',[['Demonstrating a breathing technique','Short video'],['Five tips for better sleep','Carousel'],['A quick fact with a source','Infographic'],['Advice from the school counselor','Interview clip'],['Checking what peers know','Poll'],['A sensitive scenario','Animation or cartoon']])]],
['Hooks, messages and calls to action',[
'Start with a **hook**—a question, surprising fact or relatable moment (“Ever blanked on a test you studied for?”). Follow with **one clear message** tied to your **risk or protective factor** and end with a **call to action** (“Try this tonight,” “Tag a friend who’s been there for you,” “Room 112 is open every lunch”). Keep text short and readable.',
'Use the **message map** approach: one core message supported by three short points with evidence. Repeat your core message across posts with different examples so it sticks. Judges look for clear **peer education**: after seeing the campaign, would a classmate know something new and know what to do?',
order('Building a post',['Hook','Core message tied to your factor','Supporting fact or example','Source credit','Call to action','Resource reminder'])]],
['Design principles',[
'Judges score **appearance and organization**: neat, organized and **error-free**, with information that is clear and easy to follow. Use a consistent **visual identity**—campaign name, logo, two or three colors and one or two fonts—so posts look like a set. Leave white space, use **hierarchy** (big headline, smaller details) and align elements.',
'Proofread every post for **spelling and grammar**, and have someone outside the team review it. Check that facts match your sources. Keep videos short and well lit with clear audio. A consistent look makes the campaign memorable and easier for judges to review in seven minutes.',
"Create a simple **style guide** at the start: logo, color codes, fonts, tone of voice and hashtag. Share it with every team member so posts made by different people still look like one campaign. A short campaign name or hashtag also helps peers remember and search for your content.",
sort('Good design choice?',['Good','Poor'],[['Consistent colors and fonts across posts',0],['Six fonts on one graphic',1],['Large headline with short supporting text',0],['Paragraphs of tiny text on an image',1],['Proofreading by someone outside the team',0],['Dark text on a dark background',1]])]],
['Creativity and originality',[
'**Creativity and originality** are worth **15 points**; judges want a “**wow factor**.” Ideas include a **campaign mascot**, a **themed challenge** (“7 days of connection”), **student-made music** or art, **myth-versus-fact** series, **behind-the-scenes** looks at school support services, **interactive stories** with choices, or partnering with clubs and teams for guest posts.',
'Original does not mean complicated. A simple, surprising idea executed well beats a busy campaign. You may **create original content** or **enhance something existing**, but credit sources and use only content you have the right to use. Test ideas on a few peers and refine them with your consultant.',
chk('Which idea best shows creativity while staying safe?',['A week-long “connection challenge” with daily small actions and a mascot','Copying another school’s posts','Graphic images for shock value','A long text post with no visuals'],'Original, positive and interactive.')]],
['Timeline and content calendar',[
'Teams **establish the timeline and method** for implementing the campaign, and judges score how clearly you explain them in Round Two. Build a **content calendar**: dates, platform, format, message, call to action, who creates it and who reviews it. Plan a **launch**, a steady **posting rhythm** (for example, three posts a week for four weeks) and a **wrap-up**.',
'Connect posting to **school events**—Mental Health Awareness Month (May), Suicide Prevention Awareness Month (September), exam weeks or club fairs. Leave time for **consultant review** before every post and for the **May 15** upload deadline, so finish early.',
order('Campaign timeline',['Choose topic and factors with the consultant','Conduct the needs assessment','Plan the content calendar','Create and review content','Launch and post on schedule','Measure results and prepare the upload'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Improving a weak post',{a:{text:'A draft post is a block of text titled “Mental Health Facts” with no image. (Scenario invented for practice.) What would improve it most?',choices:[['A relatable hook, one clear message tied to your factor, a visual and a call to action','b','Correct.',true],['Adding more paragraphs','b','Shorter and visual is better.',false]]},b:{text:'Your posts use different colors and fonts each time. What do you do?',choices:[['Create a consistent visual identity with a logo and set colors and fonts','c','Correct.',true],['Keep changing them for variety','c','Consistency helps recognition.',false]]},c:{text:'You map out a four-week calendar with consultant review before each post.',end:true}})]],
["Video and photo production tips",["Good production makes content easier to watch. Film in **natural light** facing a window or with a ring light, hold the phone **steady** or use a tripod, and record **vertical** video for reels and stories. Use a quiet room or a clip-on microphone; poor audio loses viewers faster than poor video.","Keep videos **short**—15 to 60 seconds for most platforms—with the main point in the first few seconds. Add **captions**, on-screen text for key points and your campaign logo. Edit out pauses and mistakes. Free editing apps can add transitions, music and text.","For photos and graphics, use free design tools with templates, but customize them with your colors and fonts. Make sure anyone shown has given permission and that the setting does not reveal private information, such as a home address or a student’s schedule.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Making a short campaign video","steps":["Write a short script with a hook","Set up light and sound","Film vertically and steadily","Edit to 15–60 seconds","Add captions, text and logo","Review with the consultant before posting"]}]]
],takeaways:['Know your peers and their platforms; choose formats to fit the message (videos, carousels, infographics, interviews, polls, animations); content should feel made for teens and stay accurate.','Each post needs a hook, one core message tied to your factor, support, source credit and a call to action; use a consistent visual identity, hierarchy, white space and error-free text.','Creativity and originality (15 points) reward a simple, surprising idea done well; plan a content calendar with dates, formats, owners and consultant review, linked to school events, finished well before May 15.'],terms:[['Hook','The opening that grabs attention.'],['Content calendar','A schedule of what will be posted, where and when.'],['Visual identity','Consistent colors, fonts and logo that make posts recognizable.']],mcqs:[q('Who is the audience for the campaign?','The team’s peers','Only judges','Only parents','Doctors','Peer education.'),q('Which format fits a step-by-step list of tips?','Carousel','Single tweet','Audio only','Long essay','Swipe through.'),q('What comes first in a strong post?','A hook','The references','A long paragraph','The logo only','Grab attention.'),q('How many points is creativity and originality worth?','15','2','50','5','Wow factor.'),q('Which is a good design choice?','Consistent colors and fonts','Six fonts on one image','Low contrast','Tiny text','Visual identity.'),q('A schedule of posts is called a:','Content calendar','Needs assessment','Reference page','Consultation form','Timeline.'),q('Judges score appearance and organization based on:','Neatness, organization and being error-free','Number of followers','Cost','Length only','Design criterion.'),q('Which month is Mental Health Awareness Month?','May','February','October','July','Link to events.')],frq:{prompt:'Plan three posts for a peer campaign on a protective factor of your choice, describing the format, hook, message and call to action for each and how they fit a timeline.',rubric:[['Factor and audience',['protective factor','peers','connectedness','coping','audience']],['Post design',['format','video','carousel','infographic','hook','message','call to action']],['Quality',['consistent','visual identity','error-free','source','accessible','captions']],['Timeline',['calendar','week','launch','schedule','consultant review','may 15']]],model:'Our protective factor is healthy coping skills for test anxiety, and our audience is students at our high school, who mostly use Instagram and TikTok. Post 1, launched the week before midterms, is a 30-second video demonstrating box breathing; the hook is “Ever blanked on a test you studied for?”, the message is that slow breathing calms the body’s stress response so you can think clearly, and the call to action is “Try four rounds tonight and save this for test day,” with a caption crediting our source and listing the counseling office and 988. Post 2, three days later, is a five-slide carousel titled “Study smarter, stress less,” with tips on sleep, breaking study time into chunks and taking movement breaks; the call to action is “Share with your study group.” Post 3, during exam week, is an interview clip with our school counselor explaining that feeling nervous is normal and how students can drop by the counseling office at lunch; the call to action is “Tag a friend who needs to hear this.” All three use our campaign logo, two colors and the same font, include captions and alt text and are proofread by someone outside the team. Our content calendar runs four weeks with three posts per week, each reviewed by our consultant two days before posting, and we will finish measuring results and building our PDF by May 1, ahead of the May 15 deadline.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_COMM,WAI]};

export const MH8={title:'The digital upload and measuring impact',hook:'A brilliant campaign earns nothing if judges cannot open it. One missing permission setting can give the entire project a zero. The Round One upload has strict requirements, and judges have only seven minutes to review it. This lesson walks through building a complete, judge-friendly PDF and showing the impact of your work.',objectives:['List every required element of the Round One upload.','Build a summary presentation that judges can review in seven minutes.','Set links to public and avoid common upload mistakes.','Measure and present campaign reach and impact.'],sections:[
['Required items and the deadline',[
'Upload **ONE COMBINED PDF FILE** to the HOSA Digital Upload System by **May 15 at midnight Eastern time**—no exceptions. If the upload is not a single PDF, judges will not view it and **no points** are awarded. **One team member** uploads it. Teams that do not upload cannot advance to Round Two. If your state conference uses the upload system, you must upload **again** for ILC.',
'The summary presentation must include: **(a)** a **title slide** with the event name, team member names, HOSA division, chapter number, school name and chartered association (one slide only); **(b)** the completed **Mental Health Consultant Form** inserted as an image; **(c)** **reference page(s)** inserted as an image (APA preferred; formatting is not judged, but they must be clean and legible); and **(d)** **examples** of the campaign with **direct links** to each platform.',
order('Building the PDF',['Title slide with all required items','Consultation form as an image','Reference page(s) as an image','Campaign examples with direct public links','Summary of topic, factors and timeline','Export to PDF and test every link'])]],
['Public links and accounts',[
'Type or paste each **URL into the PDF**, and make sure every link is set to **public**. **Links without public permission will result in the entire project receiving a zero.** Content must be viewable **without logging in**. Test every link in a private (incognito) browser window, on a phone and on a computer, and ask someone outside the team to try them.',
'Do **not** post on personal accounts. Create a **dedicated campaign account** or use your chapter’s HOSA account. Keep the account active through the competition season so links still work when judges review them.',
"If you use a video platform or cloud drive, check its sharing options carefully. Settings such as “restricted,” “private” or “only people in my organization” will block judges. Choose “anyone with the link” or “public,” then sign out and test again to be sure.",
sort('Upload ready?',['Ready','Not ready'],[['Every link opens in a private browser without logging in',0],['A Google Drive video set to “Only people in my organization”',1],['Content posted on a dedicated chapter account',0],['Content on a team member’s private personal account',1],['One combined PDF file',0],['Three separate files uploaded',1]])]],
['Designing for a seven-minute review',[
'Judges have **seven minutes**. Decide how many posts to include so there is neither **too much** nor **too little**. Lead with a one-slide **campaign overview**: topic, risk or protective factors, audience, platforms and dates. Then show your **strongest examples**, each with a short caption explaining its purpose and the factor it addresses, and the link.',
'Use the rating sheet as your checklist: clear **risk/protective factors** (15), **understanding of the topic** (15), **effectiveness and impact** (15), **peer education** (15), **appearance and organization** (10) and **creativity** (15). Make each criterion easy to find. Keep slides clean and consistent with your campaign’s visual identity.',
match('Rating-sheet criterion and how to show it',[['Risk/protective factors','Name them on the overview and in each caption'],['Understanding of topic','Accurate facts with sources'],['Effectiveness and impact','Results and reach data'],['Peer education','Content clearly made for teens'],['Appearance and organization','Clean, consistent, error-free slides'],['Creativity','Highlight your most original idea']])]],
['References and credits',[
'Compile **reference pages** citing all literature used to develop the campaign, including the suggested resources you read (such as the Surgeon General’s advisory, SAMHSA, the Jed Foundation and Be There). **APA style** is preferred in health science. A basic APA reference for a webpage lists the author or organization, year, title, site name and URL.',
'Also credit **images, music and quotes** used in posts. Keep a running list from the start of the project so you do not have to reconstruct it before the deadline. Reference pages earn **5 points** for being included, and they support your “understanding of topic” score.',
chk('What happens if a link in your upload is not set to public?',['The entire project receives a zero','Only that post loses points','Judges will request access','Nothing'],'Always test links in a private browser.')]],
['Measuring impact',[
'Show **effectiveness and impact** with evidence. **Reach** includes views, impressions and followers. **Engagement** includes likes, shares, saves, comments (if enabled) and poll participation. **Knowledge and attitude change** can be measured with short anonymous **pre- and post-polls** (“Can you name two ways to reach a counselor?”). **Feedback** from your consultant, teachers or peers adds qualitative evidence.',
'Present numbers honestly and with context: “Our five videos reached 1,200 views in a school of 900 students” is more meaningful than “1,200 views.” Remember that engagement shows **attention**, not proven behavior change. Compare results to your **SMART objectives** and explain what you learned.',
"If a result was lower than you hoped, say so and explain what you would change. Judges value honest reflection—such as “our first posts got few views, so we shifted to short videos and engagement tripled”—more than inflated numbers.",
sort('Type of evidence',['Reach','Engagement','Knowledge change'],[['Number of video views',0],['Number of shares and saves',1],['Poll: % who can name two coping strategies, before and after',2],['Followers gained during the campaign',0],['Number of students who joined a challenge',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Final check before upload',{a:{text:'It is May 10. Your team’s PDF is ready. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do before uploading?',choices:[['Open every link in a private browser on two devices and confirm the title slide has every required item','b','Correct.',true],['Upload at 11:59 p.m. on May 15','b','Upload early to avoid problems.',false]]},b:{text:'One video link asks viewers to sign in. What do you do?',choices:[['Change the sharing setting to public and retest it','c','Correct.',true],['Leave it; judges can request access','c','A non-public link can zero the project.',false]]},c:{text:'One team member uploads the single combined PDF on May 11 and saves the confirmation.',end:true}})]],
["Common upload mistakes",["The most costly mistakes are avoidable. **Non-public links** can zero the entire project. **Multiple files** instead of one combined PDF mean judges will not score it. A **missing or unsigned consultation form** loses 10 points. A title slide that is **missing required items** or uses more than one slide loses points.","Other mistakes include **too much content** for a seven-minute review, links that point to a **profile** instead of the specific post, **expired** story links, blurry screenshots and spelling errors. Some teams forget that state uploads do not count for ILC and must be repeated.","Create a **checklist** from the guidelines and rating sheet, and have someone who did not build the PDF check every item. Upload a few days early and keep the confirmation.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Mistake or best practice?","buckets":["Mistake","Best practice"],"items":[["Linking to a profile instead of the specific post",0],["Testing links in a private browser",1],["Uploading three separate files",0],["Having a non-team member check the PDF",1],["Using a story link that expires in 24 hours",0],["Uploading several days before the deadline",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Upload one combined PDF by May 15 (midnight Eastern) with a one-slide title (event, names, division, chapter number, school, chartered association), the consultation form and references as images and campaign examples with direct links; upload again for ILC if your state used the system.','Every link must be public and viewable without login—non-public links give the entire project a zero; never use personal accounts; test in a private browser on several devices.','Design for a seven-minute review using the rating sheet; include APA-preferred references and credits; show impact with reach, engagement and pre/post knowledge polls in context, compared with your SMART objectives.'],terms:[['Digital upload','The required PDF submitted through HOSA’s online system.'],['Reach','How many people saw the content.'],['Engagement','How people interacted with the content, such as shares and saves.']],mcqs:[q('What format must the Round One upload be?','One combined PDF file','Separate Word files','A slideshow file','A ZIP folder','Single PDF.'),q('What is the upload deadline?','May 15','June 30','January 1','The day of ILC','Midnight Eastern.'),q('What happens if campaign links are not public?','The entire project receives a zero','Nothing','Judges will ask for access','Only one point is lost','Test them.'),q('How many slides may the title slide use?','One','Three','Five','Unlimited','Title slide only.'),q('Which reference style is preferred?','APA','MLA only','None','Chicago only','Health science.'),q('Which is engagement?','Shares and saves','Number of team members','The PDF length','The deadline','Interaction.'),q('How long do judges have to review the upload?','Seven minutes','One minute','Thirty minutes','Two hours','Plan content.'),q('Where should campaign content be posted?','A dedicated or chapter account','A private personal account','A locked group','Email only','Not personal accounts.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would prepare the Round One digital upload so judges can easily review it and see the campaign’s impact.',rubric:[['Required items',['one pdf','title slide','event','names','division','chapter','school','chartered association','consultation form','references','image']],['Links and accounts',['public','login','private browser','test','dedicated account','zero']],['Seven-minute design',['overview','factors','examples','captions','rating sheet','organized']],['Impact',['reach','engagement','poll','pre','post','smart','context','may 15']]],model:'We would build one summary presentation and export it as a single combined PDF. The first slide would be the only title slide and include the event name, all team member names, our HOSA division, chapter number, school name and chartered association. Next we would insert the signed Mental Health Consultant Form and our APA-style reference pages as images. Then we would add a one-slide campaign overview naming our topic, the protective factor of school connectedness, our audience, platforms and posting dates, followed by our strongest posts, each with a short caption explaining its purpose and how it builds connectedness, plus a typed direct link. Because non-public links give the whole project a zero, we would post only on a dedicated campaign account and test every link in a private browser on a phone and a computer, and have someone outside the team try them. We would keep the content focused enough for the judges’ seven-minute review, using the rating sheet criteria—factors, understanding, effectiveness, peer education, design and creativity—as our checklist. To show impact, we would include reach and engagement numbers in context, such as views compared with our school’s enrollment, and results of an anonymous before-and-after poll showing how many students could name two ways to connect with a club or counselor, compared with our SMART objective. One team member would upload the PDF several days before May 15 and save the confirmation.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOADS,APA]};

export const MH9={title:'Structuring the five-minute presentation',hook:'Five minutes goes fast—especially with up to six speakers and a device to show campaign clips. Judges score seven specific content items in Round Two, and the timekeeper stops you at five minutes. A clear structure that covers every item, in order, is the difference between a rushed presentation and a winning one.',objectives:['List the six required presentation elements and how they are scored.','Build a five-minute outline that covers every element.','Use the device to show campaign pieces smoothly.','Follow the rules on notecards, devices and visual aids.'],sections:[
['What the presentation must include',[
'The presentation must: **(a)** share **key points learned** from the recommended resources; **(b)** explain the **risk and/or protective factors** the campaign addressed; **(c)** summarize **why** the topic was chosen, including the needs assessment; **(d)** outline the **timeline and methods**; **(e)** share how the **Mental Health Consultant** was used in the learning process; and **(f)** describe **next steps** and how the team could expand its impact. Judges also score how well you **incorporate the campaign** during the presentation.',
'Content scoring: learning points **10**, consultant **10**, timeline and method **10**, incorporation of the campaign **10**, why **15**, risk/protective factors **15** and next steps **15**—**85 points**. Delivery adds **20** more. The **why**, **factors** and **next steps** carry the most weight, so give them the most time.',
match('Required element and points',[['Learning points from resources','10'],['Consultant incorporation','10'],['Timeline and method','10'],['Incorporating the campaign','10'],['Why the topic was chosen','15'],['Risk/protective factors','15']])]],
['A five-minute outline',[
'A sample plan for **5:00**: **Opening and why** (0:00–1:00)—a hook, the problem at your school and your needs assessment. **Factors** (1:00–2:00)—your risk or protective factors and the evidence behind them. **Learning points and consultant** (2:00–2:45)—two or three key lessons from the resources and how the consultant shaped your work. **Campaign and timeline** (2:45–3:45)—show one or two short campaign pieces and explain the timeline and methods. **Impact and next steps** (3:45–4:45)—results and specific next steps. **Close** (4:45–5:00).',
'The timekeeper announces when **one minute** remains and stops the presentation at **five minutes**. Rehearse with a timer until you finish at about **4:45**, leaving a buffer. Anything you do not say before time is called cannot be scored.',
"Build in flexibility. Mark one or two sentences in each section as “optional” that a speaker can skip if the team is running behind. If a clip will not play, have a teammate describe it in one sentence and move on rather than spending time troubleshooting.",
order('Five-minute outline',['Hook and why (needs assessment)','Risk/protective factors and evidence','Learning points and consultant role','Campaign pieces, timeline and methods','Impact and next steps','Memorable close'])]],
['Using the device well',[
'You may use a **personal electronic device** running on **battery power** to show **parts of the campaign**; HOSA provides **no** equipment, Wi-Fi or AV. The screen must be visible to judges sitting **up to five feet** away. The presentation itself is **verbal**: do **not** create slides, a Prezi or other visual aids. Campaign pieces you show **count toward the five minutes**.',
'Download videos so they play **without Wi-Fi**, queue them in order, set brightness high and volume appropriate and charge the device fully. Show short clips (10–20 seconds) or a single strong image while a teammate explains its purpose. Practice handing the device smoothly; a fumbling transition wastes precious seconds.',
sort('Allowed in Round Two?',['Allowed','Not allowed'],[['A tablet showing a campaign video',0],['A PowerPoint summarizing the presentation',1],['Paper index cards',0],['A poster board',1],['Electronic notecards on a different device from the one shown to judges',0],['A Prezi presentation',1]])]],
['Notecards and team roles',[
'**Index-card notes** are allowed. **Electronic notecards** are allowed only on a **different device** from the one shown to judges, and they may not be shown. Use notes as reminders, not scripts; judges score **eye contact and stage presence**. Write key words, numbers and transitions in large print.',
'**Team participation** is scored: each member should speak and carry a fair share. Assign sections by strengths—one member tells the “why,” another explains the factors, another runs the device and describes the campaign. Plan **transitions** (“Now Maya will show how we put this into action”) so handoffs feel smooth.',
chk('Which use of notes is allowed?',['Index cards or electronic notes on a separate device not shown to judges','Notes displayed on the screen judges see','A slideshow of notes','Reading a full script from a phone shown to judges'],'Notes must not be shown to judges.')]],
['Explaining the why, factors and next steps',[
'For the **why** (15 points), share specific details from your needs assessment—what your consultant reported, data from your school or state and why the team cared. For the **factors** (15 points), name each risk or protective factor, explain the evidence that it matters and show how it appeared throughout the campaign. Judges want to see you have **command of the topic**.',
'For **next steps** (15 points), be **specific and realistic**: continuing the campaign next year, partnering with a club or student council, presenting to middle schools, training peers in Be There, working with administrators on a wellness space or advocating for a policy such as mental health days. Show how you would **expand** and **sustain** the impact.',
"Tie next steps to what you learned. If your poll showed students still did not know where the counseling office was, a next step could be a campus map post or a counselor “open house.”",
order('Strong next-steps statement',['Name a specific action','Who you will partner with','When it will happen','How it builds the same factor','How you will measure it','How it will continue after the team graduates'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Running out of time',{a:{text:'In rehearsal, your team reaches next steps at 4:50. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you change?',choices:[['Shorten the opening and clips so next steps (15 points) get a full minute','b','Correct.',true],['Talk faster and skip next steps','b','Next steps carry heavy weight.',false]]},b:{text:'One teammate speaks for three minutes while others barely talk. What do you do?',choices:[['Redistribute sections so every member speaks','c','Correct. Team participation is scored.',true],['Keep it; the best speaker should talk most','c','Judges score shared participation.',false]]},c:{text:'Your next rehearsal ends at 4:45 with every element covered and every member speaking.',end:true}})]],
["Opening and closing strong",["Your **opening** should grab attention and lead straight into your “why.” Options include a short, relatable scenario (“Imagine walking into a cafeteria where you don’t know anyone”), a striking statistic from your needs assessment or a quick clip from your campaign. Avoid starting with “Hi, our names are…”—you can introduce yourselves briefly after the hook.","Your **close** should restate your protective factor and your impact in one sentence and end with a memorable line or your campaign’s call to action. For example: “Connection is protection. Our campaign helped 300 freshmen find their place—and next year we’ll help 300 more.”","Rehearse the opening and close until they are word-perfect. Strong first and last impressions frame the judges’ view of everything in between.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Strong or weak opening?","buckets":["Strong","Weak"],"items":[["A relatable scenario that leads into the why",0],["“Um, so, our project is about mental health”",1],["A key number from your needs assessment",0],["Reading the title slide aloud",1],["A 10-second campaign clip that sets the tone",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Required: learning points from resources (10), consultant use (10), timeline and methods (10), incorporating the campaign (10), why with needs assessment (15), risk/protective factors (15) and next steps (15); delivery adds 20.','Plan a five-minute outline finishing about 4:45; the timekeeper warns at one minute and stops at five; shown campaign pieces count toward the time.','Use a battery-powered device visible from five feet to show only campaign pieces—no slides, Prezi or posters; index cards or notes on a separate device; every member speaks with smooth transitions; next steps must be specific and sustainable.'],terms:[['Verbal presentation','A spoken presentation without slides or visual aids.'],['Transition','A phrase that hands off smoothly to the next speaker or topic.'],['Next steps','Specific future actions to expand and sustain the campaign’s impact.']],mcqs:[q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','5 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','15 minutes','Timekeeper stops at five.'),q('Which presentation element is worth 15 points?','Next steps','Learning points','Consultant incorporation','Timeline','Also why and factors.'),q('Which visual aid is NOT allowed?','A PowerPoint','A device showing the campaign','Index cards','None of these','No slides.'),q('Electronic notecards must be on:','A different device from the one shown to judges','The same device','A projector','Paper only','Not shown to judges.'),q('Does showing campaign clips count toward the five minutes?','Yes','No','Only videos','Only images','All time counts.'),q('What does HOSA provide for Round Two?','No equipment, Wi-Fi or AV','A projector','Wi-Fi','Speakers','Bring your own battery-powered device.'),q('The screen must be visible from up to:','Five feet','Fifty feet','One foot','Across the room','Judges’ seats.'),q('Which shows strong team participation?','Every member speaks and carries a fair share','One member speaks the whole time','Members read silently','Only the leader answers','Scored item.')],frq:{prompt:'Create a five-minute outline for your team’s Round Two presentation that covers every required element and explains how you will use your device and divide speaking roles.',rubric:[['Required elements',['learning points','resources','factors','why','needs assessment','timeline','methods','consultant','next steps']],['Timing',['5 minutes','4:45','one minute','minutes','outline']],['Device and rules',['battery','five feet','campaign pieces','no slides','notecards','separate device']],['Team roles',['each member','speaks','transition','roles','participation']]],model:'Our four-member team will finish in about 4:45. From 0:00 to 1:00, Ana opens with a hook about a lonely first week of freshman year and explains our why: our counselor reported that many freshmen feel disconnected, and our anonymous survey found that over half did not know how to join a club. From 1:00 to 2:00, Ben explains our protective factor, school connectedness, and the CDC evidence that students who feel connected are less likely to report persistent sadness, showing how every post tied to connection. From 2:00 to 2:45, Chris shares key points we learned from the Surgeon General’s advisory and the Be There golden rules and how our consultant helped choose the factor, reviewed every post for safety and confirmed counseling resources. From 2:45 to 3:45, Dani holds our fully charged tablet, visible from five feet, and plays a 15-second clip from our “seven days of connection” challenge and shows one carousel, while Chris explains our four-week timeline of three posts per week on our chapter Instagram and TikTok. From 3:45 to 4:45, Ana shares results—views compared with enrollment and our before-and-after poll—and next steps: training club leaders in Be There next fall and presenting the challenge to our feeder middle school, measured with the same poll. Ben closes by 5:00. We use only the tablet for campaign pieces, no slides, and keep index cards with key numbers, with each member speaking and clear transitions between sections.'},sources:[RULES,JED,SG]};

export const MH10={title:'Delivery, next steps and sustaining the work',hook:'Judges remember teams that speak with energy, look at them and clearly care about their peers. Delivery is worth 20 points—and it also makes your content more convincing. After the competition, the best campaigns keep going. This lesson covers polished delivery, credible next steps and caring for your team while working on difficult topics.',objectives:['Apply the four delivery criteria: voice, stage presence, diction and team participation.','Rehearse effectively and handle nerves.','Plan realistic next steps that expand and sustain impact.','Practice self-care and team care during a mental health project.'],sections:[
['Voice and diction',[
'**Voice** (5 points) covers pitch, tempo, volume and quality: speak **loudly enough** to be heard, **vary** your rate and volume to emphasize key points and use **pauses**. **Diction, pronunciation and grammar** (5 points) covers word choice and clarity: enunciate, pronounce terms correctly (practice words like “psychologist”) and avoid **vocal fillers** such as “um,” “uh” and “you know.”',
'Record a rehearsal and listen for fillers and rushing. Replace fillers with a short pause. Slow down for important numbers and your call to action. A warm, sincere tone fits a mental health topic better than a sales pitch.',
"Practice difficult moments, such as saying a statistic about suicide calmly and respectfully. Speak about sensitive topics with care, using the same safe language as your campaign: “died by suicide,” “a person living with anxiety” and “help is available.” Judges notice when delivery and message match.",
sort('Strong or weak delivery?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Pausing before a key statistic',0],['Saying “um” between every sentence',1],['Varying volume to emphasize the call to action',0],['Speaking in a monotone',1],['Clear pronunciation of “psychologist”',0],['Rushing to fit everything in',1]])]],
['Stage presence and teamwork',[
'**Stage presence** (5 points) covers poise, posture, eye contact and enthusiasm. Stand tall, face the judges, make eye contact with each one and use purposeful gestures. Facial expressions should match your message—warm and engaged. Avoid fidgeting, swaying or reading from notes.',
'**Team participation** (5 points) rewards **shared collaboration**: each member speaks and carries an equal part. Team members who are not speaking should look at the speaker or judges and stay engaged. Practice handoffs until they are smooth, and agree on who answers if judges ask a question at the end (if time allows).',
match('Delivery criterion and what judges look for',[['Voice','Volume, varied rate, pauses'],['Stage presence','Posture, eye contact, enthusiasm'],['Diction','Clear words, no fillers'],['Team participation','Every member speaks equally'],['Gestures','Purposeful, not distracting'],['Tone','Sincere and caring']])]],
['Rehearsing and handling nerves',[
'Rehearse the full presentation **at least five times** with a timer, then in front of an audience—your advisor, consultant or another HOSA chapter. Practice with the actual device, the clips and your notecards. Rehearse in business attire once so nothing surprises you. Ask for feedback on the rating-sheet criteria.',
'Nerves are normal. Use the same coping skills your campaign teaches: slow breathing before you enter, a positive self-statement (“We know this topic and care about it”), and a team ritual like a quick huddle. If someone forgets a line, a teammate can smoothly continue—another reason to know each other’s sections.',
order('Rehearsal plan',['Write and time the outline','Practice each section individually','Run the full presentation with the device','Present to an audience for feedback','Revise and rehearse to about 4:45','Final run-through in business attire'])]],
['Next steps that expand and sustain impact',[
'Strong next steps are **specific**, **realistic** and connected to the **same factor**. Examples: train peer leaders with **Be There**; partner with **student council** to add a wellness corner; bring the campaign to **middle schools**; create a recurring “**check-in Tuesday**” tradition; work with administrators to post counseling information in every classroom; host a mental health awareness week in **May**; advocate for school policies that support well-being.',
'Plan for **sustainability**: recruit younger members to continue the account, document your process so future teams can build on it, and keep working with your consultant. Measure the next steps the same way you measured the campaign so you can show long-term impact.',
"Involve the people who can make next steps happen. A short meeting with your principal, student council or a club advisor can turn an idea into a plan, and mentioning that meeting in your presentation shows judges your next steps are realistic.",
sort('Strong next step?',['Specific and sustainable','Vague'],[['Training 20 club leaders in Be There next fall',0],['“We’ll keep spreading awareness”',1],['Presenting the challenge to our feeder middle school in March',0],['“Maybe do more posts someday”',1],['Handing the account to next year’s officers with a guide',0]])]],
['Caring for yourself and your team',[
'Working on mental health topics can bring up strong feelings. **Check in** with one another regularly, take breaks, set boundaries on how much time you spend reading difficult material and talk with your consultant or another trusted adult if something feels heavy. It is okay for a team member to step back from a topic that is personally hard.',
'Model the protective factors you promote: get sleep before competition, eat well, move and stay connected. Celebrate your progress. Remember that the campaign’s goal is to help peers—your team’s well-being matters just as much.',
"After the competition, hold a team debrief. Talk about what went well, what you would change and how the project affected each of you. Thank your consultant, share results with your school and decide together how the campaign will continue.",
chk('A teammate finds the topic personally upsetting while researching. What should the team do?',['Check in, let them take a different role and encourage them to talk with the consultant or a trusted adult','Tell them to push through','Remove them from the team','Ignore it'],'Model the support you promote.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Final rehearsal',{a:{text:'In your final rehearsal, judges-for-practice say your content is strong but your voices are quiet and you read from cards. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you change?',choices:[['Project your voices, use cards only for key words and make eye contact with each judge','b','Correct.',true],['Read faster to finish early','b','Delivery is scored.',false]]},b:{text:'Your next steps say, “We will keep spreading awareness.” How can you strengthen them?',choices:[['Name a specific action, partner, date and measure, such as training club leaders in Be There next fall','c','Correct.',true],['Add more enthusiasm to the same sentence','c','Specific plans score higher.',false]]},c:{text:'At ILC your team speaks clearly, shares the time evenly and closes with specific next steps.',end:true}})]],
["Reflecting on what you learned",["The first Round Two criterion asks teams to share **key points learned from the recommended resources**. Reflect as a team: What surprised you? Which protective factors did the Surgeon General’s advisory emphasize? What did Be There teach you about supporting a friend? How did the Jed Foundation or SAMHSA resources shape your messages?","Connect learning to action: “We learned that connectedness is one of the strongest protective factors, so we designed every post around helping freshmen find a club or a trusted adult.” This shows judges that you **incorporated** what you learned, which earns the highest scores.","Reflection also helps you grow as future health professionals. Many members say this event changed how they talk about mental health with friends and family—note those personal changes, too.",{"t":"check","q":"Which statement best shows that a team incorporated what it learned?","options":["“We learned connectedness is protective, so every post helped freshmen find a club or trusted adult.”","“We read some websites.”","“Mental health is important.”","“We learned a lot.”"],"why":"Link a specific lesson to a specific campaign choice."}]]
],takeaways:['Delivery (20 points): voice—volume, varied rate and pauses; stage presence—posture, eye contact and enthusiasm; diction—clear pronunciation without fillers; team participation—every member speaks equally.','Rehearse at least five times with the timer, device and notecards, then before an audience; manage nerves with breathing and positive self-talk; know one another’s sections.','Next steps must be specific, realistic, tied to the same factor and sustainable (peer training, partnerships, middle school outreach, account handoff); check in on teammates and model the protective factors you promote.'],terms:[['Vocal filler','A sound or word like “um” that fills silence.'],['Stage presence','Poise, posture, eye contact and enthusiasm while presenting.'],['Sustainability','Keeping a project’s impact going over time.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a vocal filler?','“Um”','A pause','A statistic','A gesture','Avoid fillers.'),q('How many points is delivery worth in Round Two?','20','5','85','115','Four criteria × 5.'),q('Which shows good stage presence?','Eye contact with each judge','Reading from notes the whole time','Swaying and fidgeting','Looking at the floor','Poise and engagement.'),q('Team participation is scored highest when:','Each member speaks and carries an equal part','One member speaks for everyone','No one speaks','Only the captain speaks','Shared collaboration.'),q('Which next step is strongest?','Training 20 club leaders in Be There next fall','“We’ll keep spreading awareness”','“Maybe post more”','“We’re done”','Specific and measurable.'),q('What should replace a filler word?','A short pause','A louder “um”','A joke','Skipping a section','Pause instead.'),q('Why plan for sustainability?','So the campaign’s impact continues after the team','To make the PDF longer','To impress only judges','It is not needed','Long-term impact.'),q('What should a team do if a member finds a topic upsetting?','Check in and adjust roles, involving the consultant if needed','Ignore it','Remove them','Make them present more','Team care.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would prepare to deliver an engaging Round Two presentation and describe specific, sustainable next steps for your campaign.',rubric:[['Voice and diction',['volume','rate','pause','fillers','pronunciation','tone']],['Presence and teamwork',['eye contact','posture','enthusiasm','gestures','each member','transitions']],['Rehearsal',['rehearse','timer','device','audience','feedback','nerves','breathing']],['Next steps',['specific','partner','date','measure','same factor','sustain','handoff']]],model:'To prepare, we would rehearse the full presentation at least five times with a timer, our tablet and our clips, then present to our advisor, consultant and another chapter for feedback using the rating sheet. For voice, we would speak loudly enough for the back of the room, slow down and pause before key numbers and vary our volume to emphasize our call to action, while recording rehearsals to catch and replace fillers like “um” with short pauses and practicing difficult words like “psychologist.” For stage presence, we would stand tall, make eye contact with each judge, use purposeful gestures and keep a warm, sincere tone that fits a mental health topic, using index cards only for key words. Each of our four members would speak for about a minute, with planned transitions so handoffs are smooth, and teammates not speaking would stay engaged. To manage nerves, we would use the box breathing our campaign teaches and a quick team huddle before entering. Our next steps would be specific and sustainable: next fall, we will partner with student council to train 20 club leaders in the Be There certificate so they can welcome and check on new members; in March, we will present our connection challenge to our feeder middle school; and we will hand the campaign account to next year’s HOSA officers with a written guide and keep the same before-and-after poll so we can measure whether connectedness keeps improving.'},sources:[RULES,JED,SG]};
